[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Of Men and Galaxies]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.87]Lesson 45 | 第45课
[00:02.87]Of men and galaxies | 人和星系
[00:11.20]What is the most influential factor in any human society? | 在任何人类社会中，什么是最具影响力的因素？
[00:18.75]In man's early days, competition with other creatures must have been critical. | 在人类早期，与其他生物的竞争想必是生死攸关的。
[00:25.22]But this phase of our development is now finished. | 但我们发展的这个阶段如今已经结束。
[00:28.88]Indeed, we lack practice and experience nowadays in dealing with primitive conditions. | 的确，如今我们在应付原始环境方面既缺乏实践也缺乏经验。
[00:35.97]I am sure that, without modern weapons, | 我敢肯定，如果没有现代武器，
[00:39.21]I would make a very poor show of disputing the ownership of a cave with a bear, and in this I do not think that I stand alone. | 我在跟一头熊争夺山洞所有权时定会狼狈不堪，而且我想持这种看法的不止我一人。
[00:49.65]The last creature to compete with man was the mosquito. | 最后一个与人类竞争的生物是蚊子。
[00:53.96]But even the mosquito has been subdued by attention to drainage and by chemical sprays. | 但就连蚊子也已被重视排水和化学喷洒的手段所降服。
[01:01.53]Competition between ourselves, person against person, | 我们人与人之间的竞争，人对人、
[01:06.13]community against community, still persists, however; and it is as fierce as it ever was. | 群体对群体的竞争却依然存在，而且一如既往地激烈。
[01:14.55]But the competition of man against man is not the simple process envisioned in biology. | 但人与人之间的竞争并非生物学中所设想的那种简单过程。
[01:21.74]It is not a simple competition for a fixed amount of food determined by the physical environment, | 它并不是为争夺由物质环境所决定的固定食物量的简单竞争，
[01:28.55]because the environment that determines our evolution is no longer essentially physical. | 因为决定我们进化的环境已基本上不再是物质性的了。
[01:35.73]Our environment is chiefly conditoned by the things we believe. | 我们的环境主要由我们所相信的事物所制约。
[01:41.69]Morocco and California are bits of the Earth in very similar latitudes, | 摩洛哥和加利福尼亚是地球上纬度非常相近的两块地方，
[01:47.47]both on the west coasts of continents with similar climates, and probably with rather similar natural resources. | 都位于大陆西岸，气候相似，自然资源可能也相当相似。
[01:55.79]Yet their present development is wholly different, | 然而它们目前的发展状况截然不同，
[02:00.02]not so much because of different people even, | 甚至主要不是因为人种不同，
[02:03.48]but because of the different thoughts that exist in the minds of their inhabitants. | 而是因为当地居民头脑中存在着不同的思想。
[02:09.45]This is the point I wish to emphasize. | 这正是我想强调的一点。
[02:12.44]The most important factor in our environment is the state of our own minds. | 我们环境中最重要的因素是我们自己头脑的状态。
[02:19.02]It is well known that where the white man has invaded a primitive culture, | 众所周知，在白人侵入原始文化的地方，
[02:24.02]the most destructive effects have come not from physical weapons but from ideas. | 最具破坏性的后果并非来自武器，而是来自思想。
[02:31.35]Ideas are dangerous. | 思想是危险的。
[02:34.29]The Holy Office knew this full well when it caused heretics to be burned in days gone by. | 宗教裁判所在昔日下令烧死异教徒时，对此深有体会。
[02:41.11]Indeed, the concept of free speech only exists in our modern society because when you are inside a community, | 的确，言论自由这一概念只存在于我们现代社会中，因为当你身处一个群体之内时，
[02:50.23]you are conditioned by the conventions of the community to such a degree that it is very difficult to conceive of anything really destructive. | 你受该群体惯例的制约如此之深，以至于很难构想出任何真正具有破坏性的东西。
[03:00.76]It is only someone looking on from outside that can inject the dangerous thoughts. | 只有置身局外的旁观者才能注入那些危险的思想。
[03:07.35]I do not doubt that it would be possible to inject ideas into the modern world that would utterly destroy us. | 我毫不怀疑，有可能向现代社会注入一些足以彻底毁灭我们的思想。
[03:15.04]I would like to give you an example, but fortunately I cannot do so. | 我很想给大家举个例子，但幸亏我做不到。
[03:21.17]Perhaps it will suffice to mention the nuclear bomb. | 或许提一下核弹就足够了。
[03:25.22]Imagine the effect on a reasonably advanced technological society, | 设想一下，让一个相当先进的技术社会、
[03:31.00]one that still does not possess the bomb, of making it aware of the possibility, of supplying suffcient details to enable the thing to be constructed. | 一个尚不拥有核弹的社会意识到这种可能性，并向它提供足以造出这种东西的足够细节，会产生什么效果。
[03:42.11]20 or 30 pages of information handed to any of the major world powers around the year 1925, | 在1925年前后，把二三十页的资料交给任何一个世界大国，
[03:50.06]would have been sufficient to change the course of world history. | 都足以改变世界历史的进程。
[03:54.74]It is a strange thought, but I believe a correct one, | 这是个奇怪的想法，但我认为是正确的：
[03:59.40]that twenty or thirty pages of ideas and information would be capable of turning the present-day world upside down, or even destroying it. | 二三十页的思想和资料就足以把当今世界搅得天翻地覆，甚至将其毁灭。
[04:12.66]I have often tried to conceive of what those pages might contain, | 我常常试图设想那几页纸上可能写着什么，
[04:18.18]but of course I cannot do so because I am a prisoner of the present-day world, just as all of you are. | 但我当然做不到，因为我和你们大家一样，都是当今世界的囚徒。
[04:26.81]We cannot think outside the particular patterns that our brains are conditioned to, or, | 我们无法跳出我们大脑所习惯的特定模式去思考，或者说，
[04:33.87]to be more accurate we can think only a very little way outside and then only if we are very original. | 更确切地说，我们只能超出这种模式思考一点点，而且还得极富独创性才行。
